TI  - FOOD-CONSUMPTION IN WEST AND EAST GERMANY
T2  - ERNAHRUNGS-UMSCHAU
LA  - German
AB  - Epidemiologic surveys have shown that the oversupply of energy in East Germany was even higher than in West Germany; this applies to men in particular.  The difference between actual daily food intake and the recommended nutrient supply was greater in East than in West Germany.  It was due mainly to different consumption levels for butter and sausages.  Energy intake from alcohol was highest in men from East Germany.  People in East Germany also consumed more bakery products and potatoes, but less milk.  The commercial offer of fruit and vegetables was less favourable physiologically.  A comparison of per head consumption data for cheese, eggs and confectionery yields different results which are difficult to evaluate.  This applies also to beverages altogether, of which women clearly consumed less, however.
